"""LDhybrid v1.0 -- hybrid4 limb-darkening coefficients in MLX, MPS-ATLAS Set 1 (plane-parallel; Kostogryz et al. 2022), JWST_NIRSpec_G395H_NRS1 passband.

MLX port of ldhybrid_hybrid4_mps1_nirspec_g395h_nrs1.py. Needs only mlx and numpy; the coefficient grid is
embedded. After import every function is MLX ops,
so the coefficients can be batched over any shape, differentiated with respect
to the stellar parameters (mx.grad, mx.vjp) and compiled (mx.compile) inside a
model:

    import mlx.core as mx
    from ldhybrid_hybrid4_mps1_nirspec_g395h_nrs1_mlx import coefficients, intensity
    r = coefficients(mx.array([5600.0, 5650.0]), 4.4, 0.0)
    r["coeffs"]      # (..., 4): (w1, w2, w3, w4); also r["w"]
    r["q"]           # (..., 4): (q1, q2, q3, q4), sampling coordinates in [0, 1]
    r["inside"]      # (...): inside the grid? (outside: NaN, or the nearest model)
    r["physical"]    # (...): positive and monotonic?
    intensity(r["coeffs"], mu)                  # I(mu)/I(1), shape (..., len(mu))
    from_q(q), to_q(c)                          # unit hypercube <-> coefficients

The weights are on hybrid4's curvature basis, the basis Elómë and
SquishierPlanet take: with Elómë, limb_dark="hybrid4" and u = r["w"]
(flux_dev_hybrid takes batched (n, 4) weights).

Law (hybrid4, curvature-simplex basis):
    I(mu)/I(1) = 1 - w1 (1 - mu^2) - w2 (1 - mu^2)^2 - w3 S3(mu) - w4 S4(mu)
    S3, S4 = B[k] . (1 - mu^4, (1 - mu^2)^2, P(mu; e0), P(mu; e1)), B = BASIS[2:]
    P(mu; e) = [(mu^2 + e)^-2 - (1 + e)^-2] / [e^-2 - (1 + e)^-2]
    e0, e1 = 0.028601207249486824, 0.38582417584735224  (pole ladder with gamma = 25/16)
With x = mu^2, w >= 0 and sum(w) <= 1 is exactly the set of hybrid4 profiles
with I(0) >= 0, dI/dx >= 0, d2I/dx2 <= 0, d3I/dx3 >= 0 on [0, 1]. Sampling
coordinates (the analogue of Kipping 2013's q1, q2): s = sum(w) = 1 - I(0) =
q1^(1/4), w1 = s(1 - q2^(1/3)), w2 = s q2^(1/3)(1 - q3^(1/2)),
w3 = s q2^(1/3) q3^(1/2)(1 - q4), w4 = s q2^(1/3) q3^(1/2) q4; uniform q is a
uniform prior over the region. Weights on SquishierPlanet's monomial-pole
hybrid4 shapes: w @ BASIS.

Coefficients: least squares uniform in projected radius (the transit-optimised
fit), constrained to that region. Grid: 16974 models, Teff 3500-7500 K,
log g 3.5-5.0, [M/H] -2.0 to +1.0.
lc_ppm is the light-curve error of the tabulated fits (p = 0.1).

Interpolation is linear on the Kuhn simplices of the (Teff, log g, [M/H])
tensor grid, as in ldhybrid_hybrid4_mps1_nirspec_g395h_nrs1.py and ldhybrid.interpolate: continuous and
piecewise linear in the stellar parameters (so the gradient is piecewise
constant), exact at the models, and a convex mix of four models (the law's
physical region is convex, so a mix of physical models is physical).
Outside the grid the outputs are NaN and inside is False, with no exception,
so a call can be traced; extrapolate=True uses the nearest model (in
Teff/100 K, log g/0.5, [M/H]/0.5) there instead, with zero gradient.

Precision follows the inputs, as in Elómë: float32 (mx.arrays or numpy) is
computed in float32 on the default device; anything else (Python floats,
float64) in float64 on the CPU stream, which matches ldhybrid_hybrid4_mps1_nirspec_g395h_nrs1.py
to 1e-12. float32 agrees to about 1e-6 in the coefficients. mx.grad with respect to a float64
mx.array must itself be called under mx.stream(mx.cpu) (an MLX rule). MLX
evaluates lazily, so outputs that are never used (such as physical) cost
nothing.

Generated by LDhybrid scripts/make_standalone.py from the v1.0 CDS tables.
Do not use with hybrid4 implementations that use the old alpha = 1 poles
(0.01176, 0.3041), or that take monomial-pole weights without converting.
"""

